 {'title': 'ELT Limitations', 'img': 'POH_7-71_elt', 'cite': 'AIM 6-2-4', 'code': C9,
  'bullets': ['Satellites stopped 121.5 in 2009', '121.5: needs ATC or aircraft', 'Must survive the crash', 'False alarms slow response'],
  'notes': 'A 121.5 ELT depends on someone hearing it. That can take hours. That is the biggest limitation.',
  'lp': {'teach': ['Cospas-Sarsat stopped monitoring 121.5 and 243.0 MHz in 2009. Those ELTs depend on ATC or an overflying aircraft hearing the signal (AIM 6-2-4).',
     'SAR forces wait for confirmation on 121.5/243.0 alerts, which can take hours, but can respond to 406 MHz alerts in minutes (AIM 6-2-4).',
     'An ELT helps only if it survives the crash and activates (AIM 6-2-4).',
     'False alarms cause expensive searches and can hide real signals. Monitor 121.5 before engine shutdown at the end of each flight (AIM 6-2-4).']}},
