 {'title': 'ELT Testing and Rules', 'img': 'POH_FigS1-2_elt', 'cite': 'AIM 6-2-4, 14 CFR 91.207, POH S1-10', 'code': C9,
  'bullets': ['Test first 5 minutes of hour', 'No more than 3 sweeps', 'Inspect every 12 calendar months', 'Battery: 1 hour or 50% life'],
  'notes': 'Analog ELTs: test only in the first 5 minutes after the hour, no more than three sweeps. No airborne tests.',
  'lp': {'teach': ['Analog 121.5/243 ELTs: test only during the first 5 minutes after any hour, for no more than three audible sweeps. Digital 406 MHz ELTs: test per the manufacturer. Airborne tests are not authorized (AIM 6-2-4).',
     'C172S POH: test every 100 hours, within the first 5 minutes of the hour, permitting only three emergency tones (POH S1-10).',
     '14 CFR 91.207: inspect within 12 calendar months for installation, battery corrosion, controls and crash sensor, and signal. Replace or recharge the battery after more than 1 cumulative hour of use or at 50 percent of its useful life. Mark the expiration date on the transmitter and in the maintenance record.',
     'Exceptions include training operations conducted entirely within 50 nautical miles of the airport where they began, and an ELT removed for repair for up to 90 days with a logbook entry and an "ELT not installed" placard (14 CFR 91.207).']}},
